In article <91Feb28.200143est.2086@ois.db.toronto.edu> jdd@db.toronto.edu (John DiMarco) writes:
>Nethack 3.0.10 is an admirable piece of work. Some quite clever ideas have
>been incorporated into the game by some talented hackers.

It is by far the cleverest computer game I have ever seen, and the
only one that comes even remotely close to the richness of a real
role-playing game (where a real human runs things).

Recently, I've seen many long lists of suggestion for NetHack 3.1
posted to rec.games.hack.  Most of these appear to me not too well
thought out; just a random stream-of-consciousness of some player
thinking of various new ideas without giving any thought to how they
fit into the game.
Many of the suggestions posted are clever, cute, or interesting.  But
many of them include modern items or concepts (NetHack should keep
it's ancient/mideval flavor).  Others make add boredom to the game, or
make it less palayable.  Some seek to change the game into something
totally different.
Most of these suggestions, if incorporated into the game, would cause
me to stick to NetHack 3.0 forever.

>However...
>Some of Nethack's ideas have not been fully developed, and they often
>lack consistency. Here are a few suggestions for improvements:

Imagine my surprise when I read this list of lucid, well thought-out
suggestions!  They all fit, they all improve the game in some minor
way, and they do not detract from something I find fun.

I do have some comments on a very few of them:

>Character classes: 
>
>Why do barbarians have poison resistance? Barbarians are good fighters, 
>admittedly, but shouldn't have any intrinsic capabilities short of their
>ability to recognize weapons.

It's another aspect of their exteremely high constitution.  I see
nothing wrong with it.

>Valkyries are good, except I don't think they should start with stealth.
>Give them stealth at level 10 or 15. 

Make it earlier.  By level 10, who needs stealth?  You've probably
gotten it by some other method by then.

>Praying and deities:
>
>Deities should be more responsive to your immediate problems when praying,
>esp. low hit points.
>
>#pray should offer a secondary menu, something like:
> Petition/Repent/Worship [P|R|W]?
>Petition should behave like the current #pray. Repent should reconcile
>an angry god (but the god might impose a penance, like an iron ball, 
>reduced hit points, or reduced luck). Repent should do nothing if the
>god isn't angry. Worship should do little or nothing, except at an altar, 
>where it might cure hit points. Worshipping at an altar of another god
>should anger one's own god, as should behaviour out of alignment. 

Hmmm...  seems like a bit too much complexity.  Can this functionality
be added in a simpler way?

>Intrinsics:
>
>You should be capable of gaining a "resist stoning" intrinsic, perhaps from
>eating a yellow dragon or wearing yellow dragon scale mail. 

Why is stoning intrinsically :) different from other kinds of
physical attacks?

>Spells:
>
>Spells should not wear out. Why should frequent use cause a spell to be
>forgotten? 

This only happens if HARD is defined.  If HARD is not defined at
compile-time, spells last indefinitely (as they used to in NetHack 1.4)

>To keep spells from becoming too powerful, make their cost in energy points
>much, much higher. 

I think spells should wear out.  It comes from precedent set by many
role-playing games, and makes some sort of 'sense'.  I remember
NetHack 1.4, where if you found spellbooks of a spell you already had,
they were totally useless.  I didn't like that.  Maybe more uses of
each spell per book would be nice, though.  And if spells still wear
out, there's no need to increase their cost in energy points.

>Amulets:
>
>The "amulet of change" should be "amulet of polymorph" and should give
>the player a "polymorph" intrinsic while worn. Make it cursed, usually. 

More amulets in general might be nice.  I can't think of any right now.

>Intrinsics:
(no, attributes :)
>
>An intrinsic range of 1-99 makes a lot more sense than AD&D's confusing
>3-18-18/** scheme. 
See below:
>Armor:
>
>Gauntlets of power should work like gauntlets of dexterity: add their
>enchantment level to your strength.

I put these two together to make you see why there's a problem here:
gauntlets of strength were made the way they were to keep them from
being useless with 18/?? strength.
Changing to a 1-99 scheme would take way too much work, refiguring
probabilities in various routines.  Besides, lots of people (me
included) are used to this system (AD&D is the most common
role-playing game).

I wouldn't mind removing the percentage strengths, though.  Make
strength be the same as all the other attributes.  Your actual
abilities should all range from 3-18, but magic items (rings of
protection, gauntlets of power, etc.) should be able to push your
abilities beyond 18 or below 3.

>Potions:
>
>A blessed potion of blindness should ask for a number of turns of blindness,
>much like a blessed potion of levitation. 
>A blessed potion of hallucination should have little or no effect.
>A blessed potion of paralysis should have little or no effect. 

Give all these potions the same behavior.  "How long do you want to be
paralysed for?" etc.

>Scrolls:
>
>A cursed scroll of charging should reduce charges to 0 (or even -1). 

Take it to -1 if it's already at 0 (should be pretty often, since it's
the items at 0 that people try to charge, usually.)

>How about a scroll of stair detection?

A poor man's magic mapping?  How is this useful?

>Wands:
>
>Wands of polymorph shouldn't polymorph more than 20ish items. 

Why do people keep attacking polypiling?  It's difficult to set up,
and you get your reward.  I think it's a valid part of the game.

>I welcome any comments.

Well, those are mine.  Apart from the parts I quoted, I fully agree
with all that you said.  I second your suggestions.  Thank you for
taking some thought, and may your posting be an example to others.

